<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lake Peipus</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lake Peipus is a large lake on the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it marked the frontier between the lands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonians">Estonians</a>, conquered by the Livonian crusaders in the 13th century, and the Novgorod Republic. On its frozen surface <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-nevsky">Alexander Nevsky</a> defeated the Livonian Order in 1242 at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-on-the-ice">Battle on the Ice</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lake-peipus.jpg" alt="Lake Peipus" width="320" height="545" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lake_Peipus,_LandSat-8,_2016-10-20.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lake Peipus is the largest trans-boundary lake in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the fifth-largest lake in Europe, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-ladoga">Lake Ladoga</a>, Lake Onega, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-vanern">Lake Vänern</a> and Lake Saimaa. It covers 3,555 square kilometres and has an average depth of 7.1 metres, the deepest point being about 15 metres. It consists of three parts: the northern Lake Peipus proper, which holds 73 per cent of the area, the southern Lake Pskov, and the sound called Warm Lake that connects them. About 30 rivers and streams flow into it, the largest being the Velikaya and the Emajõgi, and it drains into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-finland">Gulf of Finland</a> through the Narva River.</p><p>The origin of the name Peipus is uncertain. The Russian name, Chudskoye ozero, means Chud Lake; the ethnonym Chud referred to various <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finnic-peoples">Finnic peoples</a> in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karelia">Karelia</a> and north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. The lake is a remnant of a larger body of water left by the last glaciation, the Weichselian, which ended about 12,000 years ago. It freezes from late November or December and thaws in April or early May.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>On 5 April 1242 the frozen southern part of the lake was the site of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-on-the-ice">Battle on the Ice</a>, also called the Battle of Lake Peipus, in which Novgorod troops led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-nevsky">Alexander Nevsky</a> defeated an army of crusading knights. The battle was unusual in being mostly fought on the frozen surface of the lake.</p><p>The largest city on the lake, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pskov">Pskov</a>, stands on the Velikaya River about 10 kilometres from the shore. It is one of the oldest cities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, known since at least 903 from a record in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Primary Chronicle</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laurentian-codex">Laurentian Codex</a>. It kept a certain independence while dominated by its neighbours Novgorod, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> and Muscovy, and was eventually incorporated into the Russian state. Its monuments include the Mirozhsky Monastery, founded in 1156 and holding frescoes of the 14th to 17th centuries, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pskov-krom">Pskov Kremlin</a>, of the 14th to 17th centuries.</p><p>Gdov was founded in 1431 as a fortress and became a city in 1780. Kallaste, on the Estonian shore, was founded in the 18th century by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-believers">Old Believers</a> who had fled from the Novgorod area, and it still has a Russian Orthodox Old-Rite church.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The lake is used for fishing, navigation and recreation. Its shallow shores are lined with marshes that flood in spring, and its 29 islands include Kolpina, Piirissaar and Kamenka. It is a stopover for migrating swans, geese and ducks, among them Bewick's swan.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lake Peipus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Prince Alexander Yaroslavich defeated a Swedish force on the Neva in 1240, earning the name Nevsky, and in 1242 defeated the Livonian branch of the Teutonic Order on the ice of <b>Lake Peipus</b>, the Battle on the Ice.</blockquote><a href="/empire/novgorod-republic">Novgorod Republic · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After that the grand princes fought chiefly as agents or rivals under Mongol overlordship, while Alexander Nevsky, before his accession, had defeated the Swedes on the Neva in 1240 and the Teutonic Knights on <b>Lake Peipus</b> in 1242.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vladimir-suzdal">Vladimir-Suzdal · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Otepää, whose name means 'bear's head', was its main stronghold, and Tartu on the Emajõgi river guarded the waterway east to <b>Lake Peipus</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/ugandi">Ugandi</a></li><li><blockquote>Vaiga, called Waiga in Latin sources, lay north of Tartu between the Emajõgi river and <b>Lake Peipus</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/vaiga">Vaiga</a></li></ul></section>
